            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Edward Witten</strong><br>\nEdward Witten was born on August 26, 1951, in Baltimore, Maryland. He developed an interest in history and linguistics during his early education but eventually turned his attention to the field of mathematics. Witten attended Brandeis University, where he received his bachelor's degree in history with a minor in linguistics. Despite this, his deep interest in mathematical theories led him to pursue a career in theoretical physics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Transition to Physics</strong><br>\nAfter initially planning to become a journalist, Witten's passion for mathematics took over, and he decided to delve into theoretical physics. He enrolled in applied mathematics at Princeton University, quickly transitioning to pure mathematics and eventually to physics. This switch was fueled by his realization of the profound connection between abstract mathematical concepts and the physical world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Contribution to String Theory</strong><br>\nWitten's most significant contribution has been to string theory, a theoretical framework in which the point-like particles of particle physics are replaced by one-dimensional objects called strings. He has helped to bridge gaps between physics and mathematics, showing that strings could provide a consistent explanation for all known forces and types of matter in the universe.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>M-Theory Pioneer</strong><br>\nWitten is also credited with being one of the leading pioneers of M-theory, which is an extension of string theory. In 1995, he proposed the existence of an eleven-dimensional universe, which encompasses various string theories. M-theory attempts to unify quantum mechanics with Einstein's theory of relativity and has revolutionized how scientists understand the fabric of space-time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Fields Medal Winner</strong><br>\nIn recognition of his work blending mathematics with theoretical physics, Witten received the prestigious Fields Medal in 1990. He is the first physicist to win this award, which is often equated with the Nobel Prize and is given to mathematicians under the age of 40 who have made significant contributions to the field.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Edward Witten at the Institute for Advanced Study</strong><br>\nEdward Witten has spent a significant portion of his career at the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton, New Jersey. This institution is renowned for its research and academic freedom, and it was once home to Albert Einstein. Witten's work here has involved deep explorations into quantum field theory, supersymmetry, and other areas that push the boundaries of our understanding of the universe.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
